Interviewer: What was your first impression when you opened the UQ Club platform?
Interviewee: My first impression was really positive. The platform uses a consistent purple theme linked to UQ's branding, which feels familiar and official right away. The homepage layout is clean and uncluttered with clear section labels. So I could instantly understand what the site is for at a glance.

Interviewer: OK, and how easy was it to find a club that matched your interest?
Interviewee: It was fairly straightforward to locate clubs suited to my hobbies and study background. Recommended clubs are displayed directly on the home page, covering sports, academic and cultural groups. So I don't need to search blindly from the start.

Interviewer: OK, sure. And did the search function and club categories help you discover relevant clubs?
Interviewee: Absolutely. The four main category filters including sports, faculty, culture and others split all clubs into logical groups, letting me narrow down options quickly. The top search bar also offers a direct way to look for a specific club name if I already know what I want to find.

Interviewer: OK, and was there any part of the interface that you found confusing or difficult to use?
Interviewee: I didn't encounter any confusing elements during my use. The navigation bar with home, clubs and profile is fixed at the top of every page, and each button's purpose is obvious. Pop-up detail windows for clubs are also simple to open without complicated steps.

Interviewer: OK, and did the club detail page provide enough information for you to decide whether to join a club?
Interviewee: Yes, the detail pop-up gives sufficient information. It introduces the club's main goals, regular weekly activities, meeting location and contact email, plus the total number of members. This information lets me judge if the club events and community fit my expectations before joining.

Interviewer: OK. And how easy was it to navigate between different pages and features on the platform?
Interviewee: Navigation is very smooth. I can switch between the homepage, full club list and personal profile with one click using the top menu. Moving between different club pages only requires simple clicks, with no slow loading or complicated menus.

Interviewer: OK, sure. And what information or features do you think are missing for the platform?
Interviewee: Several useful functions are absent. There's no option to view upcoming events or a calendar for each club, nor reviews and feedback from current members. Also, there is no one-click join button and no photo galleries to learn more about their events.

Interviewer: OK, I understand. How useful do you think this platform would be for new UQ students looking to get involved on campus?
Interviewee: This platform would be extremely helpful for new students. It gathers all university clubs in one central place. Freshmen can browse clubs by categories based on their interests, learn about cultural and academic communities, and avoid the stress of searching for clubs across many separate websites.

Interviewer: Compared with social media and asking friends, how does this platform perform?
Interviewee: It outperforms other methods in convenience and organization. Official UQ websites often hide club information deep inside different pages. Social media posts are disorganized and easy to miss. Recommendations from friends are limited to the clubs they know. This platform organizes all clubs systematically, making it far easier to explore all choices independently.

Interviewer: OK, understand. If this platform were officially available, how likely would you want to use it in the future and why?
Interviewee: I would be very likely to use this platform regularly. As a UQ student, I want to explore campus clubs that match my study and life interests. This platform collects all options in one simple interface. I could easily check new clubs and activity details, and manage my joined clubs from my profile page. It saves a lot of time compared to searching through scattered channels.

